investigators are working to find out  why a World War 2 era plane crash that  Connecticut's largest airport killing at  least seven people the b-17 bomber was  trying to make an emergency landing at  Bradley International Airport after  engine problems ten passengers and three  crew members were on board Kris Van  Cleave is at the airport Kris what more  do we know about the accident  well good morning this b-17 was a bit of  a flying museum that was on a national  tour I'm gonna step out of the way I  want you to look at this runway right  back here it's runway 6 the NTSB is  going to be looking very closely at it  because that's where the plane came down  before it veered to the right eventually  hitting a building we can pan over you  can see the tail of the plane is right  next to a dump truck they're in the shot  now there were six people on board who  survived three were critically hurt two  others on the ground including a  firefighter were also injured dark black  smoke filled the Connecticut sky moments  after the 75 year old b-17 bomber  crash-landed off the runway at Bradley  Airport with 13 people on board I  thought boy I hope it makes the airport  Claire Palmer saw the plane moments  before the crash it was sputtering and  popping noise and I thought what's the  matter with that engine the four engine  propeller driven plane took off at 9:45  Wednesday morning just five minutes  later the pilots made that emergency  call and at 954 they landed short of the  runway at Bradley and crashed into a  nearby de-icing facility and maintenance  building the left wing is broken off and  there are various pieces of plane across  the facility  Jennifer Hamid II is with the NTSB are  you surprised people survived this crash  this was a significant tragedy and from  the scene I am surprised that people  survived  Robert Riedel a world war two buff is  one of the passengers who died in the  crash they paid as much as four hundred  and fifty dollars for a 30-minute ride  Wednesday Riedel posted this picture  from inside the doomed plane his family  said this flight was on his bucket list  Reidel son told us at least he was doing  something he loved now there are only  about 10 flying b-17s left they  typically don't have black boxes so the  NTSB will be looking for any electronics  cameras cell phones anything that may  have some information to help them  understand what happened onboard Gayle  Chris that last picture tough to see  thank you very much
 

